.SH "NAME"
pcre2syntax \- PCRE2 syntax quick reference
.SH "PCRE2 REGULAR EXPRESSION SYNTAX SUMMARY"
.sp
The full syntax and semantics of the regular expression patterns that are
supported by PCRE2 are described in the
\f(CRpcre2pattern\fP
documentation. This document contains a quick\-reference summary of the pattern
syntax followed by the syntax of replacement strings in substitution function.
The full description of the latter is in the
\f(CRpcre2api\fP
documentation.
.SH "QUOTING"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rs\fP\f(CIx\fP
.ad l
where \fIx\fP is non\-alphanumeric, matches a literal \fIx\fP
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP
.ad l
treat enclosed characters as literal
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Note that white space inside \f(CI\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP is always treated as literal, even if
\f(CBPCRE2_EXTENDED\fP is set, causing most other white space to be ignored. Note also
that PCRE2\(cqs handling of \f(CI\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP has some differences from Perl\(cqs. See the
\f(CRpcre2pattern\fP
documentation for details.
.SH "BRACED ITEMS"
.sp
With one exception, wherever brace characters \f(CI{\fP and \f(CI}\fP are required to enclose
data for constructions such as \f(CI\(rsg{2}\fP or \f(CI\(rsk{name}\fP, space and/or horizontal tab
characters that follow \f(CI{\fP or precede \f(CI}\fP are allowed and are ignored. In the case
of quantifiers, they may also appear before or after the comma. The exception
is \f(CI\(rsu{...}\fP which is not Perl\-compatible and is recognized only when
\f(CBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP is set. This is an ECMAScript compatibility feature, and
follows ECMAScript\(cqs behaviour.
.SH "ESCAPED CHARACTERS"
.sp
This table applies to ASCII and Unicode environments. An unrecognized escape
sequence causes an error.
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsa\fP
.ad l
alarm, that is, the BEL character (\f(CR0x07\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsc\fP\f(CIx\fP
.ad l
"control\-\fIx\fP", where \fIx\fP is a non\-control ASCII character
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rse\fP
.ad l
escape (\f(CR0x1B\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsf\fP
.ad l
form feed (\f(CR0x0C\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsn\fP
.ad l
newline (\f(CR0x0A\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsr\fP
.ad l
carriage return (\f(CR0x0D\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rst\fP
.ad l
tab (\f(CR0x09\fP)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rs0\fP\f(CIdd\fP
.ad l
character with octal code 0\fIdd\fP
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rs\fP\f(CIddd\fP
.ad l
character with octal code \fIddd\fP, or backreference
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rso{\fP\f(CIddd...\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
character with octal code \fIddd...\fP
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsN{U+\fP\f(CIhh...\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
character with Unicode code point \fIhh...\fP (Unicode mode only)
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsx\fP\f(CIhh\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhh\fP
.TP 21
\f(CI\(rsx{\fP\f(CIhh...\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhh...\fP
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
\f(CI\(rsN{U+\fP\f(CIhh...\fP\f(CI}\fP is synonymous with \f(CI\(rsx{\fP\f(CIhh...\fP\f(CI}\fP but is not supported in environments
that use EBCDIC code (mainly IBM mainframes). Note that \f(CI\(rsN\fP not followed by an
opening curly bracket has a different meaning (see below).
.sp
If \f(CBPCRE2_ALT_BSUX\fP or \f(CBPCRE2_EXTRA_ALT_BSUX\fP is set ("ALT_BSUX mode"), the
following are also recognized:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 14
\f(CI\(rsU\fP
.ad l
the character "U"
.TP 14
\f(CI\(rsu\fP\f(CIhhhh\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhhhh\fP
.TP 14
\f(CI\(rsu{\fP\f(CIhh...\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
character with hex code \fIhh...\fP but only for EXTRA_ALT_BSUX
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
When \f(CI\(rsx\fP is not followed by \f(CI{\fP, one or two hexadecimal digits are read,
but in ALT_BSUX mode \f(CI\(rsx\fP must be followed by two hexadecimal digits to be
recognized as a hexadecimal escape; otherwise it matches a literal \f(CIx\fP.
Likewise, if \f(CI\(rsu\fP (in ALT_BSUX mode) is not followed by four hexadecimal digits
or (in EXTRA_ALT_BSUX mode) a sequence of hex digits in curly brackets, it
matches a literal \f(CIu\fP.
.sp
Note that \f(CI\(rs0\fP\f(CIdd\fP is always an octal code. The treatment of backslash followed by
a non\-zero digit is complicated; for details see the section
Non\-printing characters
in the
\f(CRpcre2pattern\fP
documentation, where details of escape processing in EBCDIC environments are
also given.
.SH "CHARACTER TYPES"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 11
\f(CI.\fP
.ad l
any character except newline; in dotall mode, any character whatsoever
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsC\fP
.ad l
one code unit, even in UTF mode (best avoided)
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsd\fP
.ad l
a decimal digit
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsD\fP
.ad l
a character that is not a decimal digit
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsh\fP
.ad l
a horizontal white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsH\fP
.ad l
a character that is not a horizontal white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsN\fP
.ad l
a character that is not a newline
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsp{\fP\f(CIxx\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
a character with the \fIxx\fP property
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsP{\fP\f(CIxx\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
a character without the \fIxx\fP property
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsR\fP
.ad l
a newline sequence
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rss\fP
.ad l
a white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsS\fP
.ad l
a character that is not a white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsv\fP
.ad l
a vertical white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsV\fP
.ad l
a character that is not a vertical white space character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsw\fP
.ad l
a "word" character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsW\fP
.ad l
a "non\-word" character
.TP 11
\f(CI\(rsX\fP
.ad l
a Unicode extended grapheme cluster
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
\f(CI\(rsC\fP is dangerous because it may leave the current matching point in the middle
of a UTF\-8 or UTF\-16 character. The application can lock out the use of \f(CI\(rsC\fP by
setting the \f(CBPCRE2_NEVER_BACKSLASH_C\fP option. It is also possible to build PCRE2
with the use of \f(CI\(rsC\fP permanently disabled.
.sp
By default, \f(CI\(rsd\fP, \f(CI\(rss\fP, and \f(CI\(rsw\fP match only ASCII characters, even in UTF\-8 mode
or in the 16\-bit and 32\-bit libraries. However, if locale\-specific matching is
happening, \f(CI\(rss\fP and \f(CI\(rsw\fP may also match characters with code points in the range
128\(en255. If the \f(CBPCRE2_UCP\fP option is set, the behaviour of these escape
sequences is changed to use Unicode properties and they match many more
characters, but there are some option settings that can restrict individual
sequences to matching only ASCII characters.
.sp
Property descriptions in \f(CI\(rsp\fP and \f(CI\(rsP\fP are matched caselessly; hyphens,
underscores, and ASCII white space characters are ignored, in accordance with
Unicode\(cqs "loose matching" rules. For example, \f(CI\(rsp{Bidi_Class=al}\fP is the same
as \f(CI\(rsp{ bidi class = AL }\fP.
