.SH "PCRE2 SPECIAL CATEGORY PROPERTIES FOR \f(CI\(rsp\fP AND \f(CI\(rsP\fP"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 7
\f(CIXan\fP
.ad l
Alphanumeric: union of properties \f(CIL\fP and \f(CIN\fP
.TP 7
\f(CIXps\fP
.ad l
POSIX space: property \f(CIZ\fP or tab, NL, VT, FF, CR
.TP 7
\f(CIXsp\fP
.ad l
Perl space: property \f(CIZ\fP or tab, NL, VT, FF, CR
.TP 7
\f(CIXuc\fP
.ad l
Universally\-named character: one that can be represented by a
Universal Character Name
.TP 7
\f(CIXwd\fP
.ad l
Perl word: property \f(CIXan\fP or underscore
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Perl and POSIX space are now the same. Perl added VT to its space character set
at release 5.18.
.SH "BINARY PROPERTIES FOR \f(CI\(rsp\fP AND \f(CI\(rsP\fP"
.sp
Unicode defines a number of binary properties, that is, properties whose only
values are true or false. You can obtain a list of those that are recognized by
\f(CI\(rsp\fP and \f(CI\(rsP\fP, along with their abbreviations, by running this command:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CRpcre2test \-LP\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
.SH "SCRIPT MATCHING WITH \f(CI\(rsp\fP AND \f(CI\(rsP\fP"
.sp
Many script names and their 4\-letter abbreviations are recognized in
\f(CI\(rsp{sc:...}\fP or \f(CI\(rsp{scx:...}\fP items, or on their own with \f(CI\(rsp\fP (and also \f(CI\(rsP\fP of
course). You can obtain a list of these scripts by running this command:
.sp
.ss \n[.ss]
.nf
.fam C
\f(CRpcre2test \-LS\fP
.fam
.fi
.ss \n[.ss] 0
