.TP 7
\f(CIword\fP
.ad l
Same as \f(CI\(rsw\fP
.TP 7
\f(CIxdigit\fP
.ad l
Hexadecimal digit
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
In PCRE2, POSIX character set names recognize only ASCII characters by default,
but some of them use Unicode properties if \f(CBPCRE2_UCP\fP is set. You can use
\f(CI\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP inside a character class.
.sp
When \f(CBPCRE2_ALT_EXTENDED_CLASS\fP is set, UTS#18 extended character classes may be
used, allowing nested character classes, combined using set operators.
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 18
\f(CI[x&&[^y]]\fP
.ad l
UTS#18 extended character class
.TP 18
\f(CIx||y\fP
.ad l
Set union (OR)
.TP 18
\f(CIx&&y\fP
.ad l
Set intersection (AND)
.TP 18
\f(CIx\-\-y\fP
.ad l
Set difference (AND NOT)
.TP 18
\f(CIx~~y\fP
.ad l
Set symmetric difference (XOR)
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.SH "PERL EXTENDED CHARACTER CLASSES"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[...])\fP
.ad l
Perl extended character class
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[\(rsp{Thai} & \(rsp{Nd}])\fP
.ad l
Operators; white space ignored
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[(\fP\f(CIX\fP\f(CI \- \fP\f(CIY\fP\f(CI) & \fP\f(CIZ\fP\f(CI])\fP
.ad l
Parentheses for grouping
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[ [^3] & \(rsp{Nd} ])\fP
.ad l
\f(CI[ ... ]\fP is a nested ordinary class
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[ [:alpha:] \- [z] ])\fP
.ad l
POSIX set is allowed outside \f(CI[ ... ]\fP but character literal must be enclosed
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[ \(rsd \- [3] ])\fP
.ad l
Backslash\-escaped set is allowed outside \f(CI[ ... ]\fP
.TP 25
\f(CI(?[ !\(rsn & [:ascii:] ])\fP
.ad l
Backslash\-escaped character is allowed outside
\f(CI[ ... ]\fP; all other characters or ranges must be enclosed in \f(CI[ ... ]\fP
.TP 25
\f(CIx|y\fP, \f(CIx+y\fP
.ad l
Set union (OR)
.TP 25
\f(CIx&y\fP
.ad l
Set intersection (AND)
.TP 25
\f(CIx\-y\fP
.ad l
Set difference (AND NOT)
.TP 25
\f(CIx^y\fP
.ad l
Set symmetric difference (XOR)
.TP 25
\f(CI!x\fP
.ad l
Set complement (NOT)
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
Inside a Perl extended character class, \f(CI[...]\fP switches mode to be interpreted
as an ordinary character class. Outside of a nested \f(CI[...]\fP, the only items
permitted are backslash\-escapes, POSIX sets, operators, and parentheses. Inside
a nested ordinary class, \f(CI^\fP has its usual meaning (inverts the class when used
as the first character); outside of a nested class, \f(CI^\fP is the XOR operator.
