Advance to next starting character
.TP 13
\f(CI(*SKIP)\fP
.ad l
Advance to current matching position
.TP 13
\f(CI(*SKIP:\fP\f(CINAME\fP\f(CI)\fP
.ad l
Advance to position corresponding to an earlier
\f(CI(*MARK:\fP\f(CINAME\fP\f(CI)\fP; if not found, the \f(CI(*SKIP)\fP is ignored
.TP 13
\f(CI(*THEN)\fP
.ad l
Local failure, backtrack to next alternation
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The effect of one of these verbs in a group called as a subroutine is confined
to the subroutine call.
.SH "CALLOUTS"
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 11
\f(CI(?C)\fP
.ad l
Callout (assumed number 0)
.TP 11
\f(CI(?C\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI)\fP
.ad l
Callout with numerical data \fIn\fP
.TP 11
\f(CI(?C"\fP\f(CItext\fP\f(CI")\fP
.ad l
Callout with string data
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The allowed string delimiters are \f(CI\*(Aq\fP, \f(CI"\fP, \f(CI^\fP,
\f(CI%\fP, \f(CI#\fP, and \f(CI$\fP (which are the same for the start
and the end), and the starting delimiter \f(CI{\fP matched with the ending
delimiter \f(CI}\fP. To encode the ending delimiter within the string, double it.
.SH "REPLACEMENT STRINGS"
.sp
If the \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_LITERAL\fP option is set, a replacement string for
\f(CBpcre2_substitute()\fP is not interpreted. Otherwise, by default, the only
special character is the dollar character in one of the following forms:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 19
\f(CI$$\fP
.ad l
Insert a dollar character
.TP 19
\f(CI$\fP\f(CIn\fP or \f(CI${\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
Insert the contents of group \fIn\fP
.TP 19
\f(CI$<\fP\f(CIname\fP\f(CI>\fP
.ad l
Insert the contents of named group
.TP 19
\f(CI$0\fP or \f(CI$&\fP
.ad l
Insert the entire matched substring
.TP 19
\f(CI$`\fP
.ad l
Insert the substring that precedes the match
.TP 19
\f(CI$\*(Aq\fP
.ad l
Insert the substring that follows the match
.TP 19
\f(CI$_\fP
.ad l
Insert the entire input string
.TP 19
\f(CI$+\fP
.ad l
Insert the highest\-numbered capture group which matched
.TP 19
\f(CI$*MARK\fP or \f(CI${*MARK}\fP
.ad l
Insert a control verb name
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
For \f(CI${\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI}\fP, \fIn\fP can be a name or a number. If \f(CBPCRE2_SUBSTITUTE_EXTENDED\fP is set,
there is additional interpretation:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 1.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " 1." 4.2
.\}
Backslash is an escape character, and the forms described in "ESCAPED
CHARACTERS" above are recognized. Also:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsQ...\(rsE\fP
.ad l
Suppress interpretation
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsl\fP
.ad l
Force the next character to lower case
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsu\fP
.ad l
Force the next character to upper case
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsL\fP
.ad l
Force subsequent characters to lower case
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsU\fP
.ad l
Force subsequent characters to upper case
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsu\(rsL\fP
.ad l
Force next character to upper case, then all lower
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsl\(rsU\fP
.ad l
Force next character to lower case, then all upper
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsE\fP
.ad l
End \f(CI\(rsL\fP or \f(CI\(rsU\fP case forcing
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsb\fP
.ad l
Backspace character (note: as in character class in pattern)
.TP 8
\f(CI\(rsv\fP
.ad l
Vertical tab character (note: not the same as in a pattern)
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 2.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " 2." 4.2
.\}
The Python form \f(CI\(rsg<\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI>\fP, where the angle brackets are part of the syntax and
\fIn\fP is either a group name or a number, is recognized as an alternative way
of inserting the contents of a group, for example \f(CI\(rsg<3>\fP.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04' 3.\h'+01'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.  sp -1
.  IP " 3." 4.2
.\}
Capture substitution supports the following additional forms:
.sp
.RS 4
.PD 0
.TP 22
\f(CI${\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI:\-\fP\f(CIstring\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
Default for unset group
.TP 22
\f(CI${\fP\f(CIn\fP\f(CI:+\fP\f(CIstring1\fP\f(CI:\fP\f(CIstring2\fP\f(CI}\fP
.ad l
Values for set/unset group
.PD
.RE
.PP
.ad l
.sp
The substitution strings themselves are expanded. Backslash can be used to
escape colons and closing curly brackets.
.RE
